For the purposes of this Decision, the following definitions shall apply:

1. **Coal** shall mean any of the following:  
   (a) Hard coal, including the high and medium-ranking ‘A’ coals (sub-bituminous coals) as defined in the international codification system of coal of the UN Economic Commission for Europe;  
   (b) Hard coal briquette;  
   (c) Coke and semi-coke derived from hard coal;  
   (d) Lignite, including the low-ranking ‘C’ coals (or ortho-lignites) and the low-ranking ‘B’ coals (or meta-lignites) as defined in the above codification;  
   (e) Lignite briquettes;  
   (f) Coke and semi-coke derived from lignite;  
   (g) Oil shales.

2. **Steel** shall mean any of the following:  
   (a) Raw materials for iron and steel production, such as iron-ore, sponge iron and ferrous scrap;  
   (b) Pig iron (including hot metal) and ferro-alloys;  
   (c) Crude and semi-finished products of iron, ordinary steel or special steel (including products for reuse and re-rolling), such as liquid steel cast by continuous casting or otherwise, and semi-finished products such as blooms, billets, bars, slabs and strips;  
   (d) Hot-finished products of iron, ordinary steel or special steel (coated or uncoated products, excluding steel castings, forgings and powder metallurgy products), such as rails, sheet piling, structural shapes, bars, wire rods, plates and universal plates, strips and sheets, and tube rounds and squares;  
   (e) End products of iron, ordinary steel or special steel (coated or uncoated), such as cold-rolled strips and sheets and electrical sheets;  
   (f) Products of the first-stage processing of steel that can enhance the competitive position of the above iron and steel products, such as tubular products, drawn and polished products, cold-rolled and cold-formed products.

Article 24 outlines the scope of implementing acts that the Commission shall adopt to ensure the effective functioning of the system of interconnection of registers. These implementing acts cover a comprehensive range of technical specifications and operational details, including:

(a) Defining the methods of electronic communication for interconnecting registers;  
(b) Specifying the communication protocols to be used;  
(c) Establishing minimum IT security standards for communication and information distribution within the system;  
(d) Defining methods for exchanging information between the company register and the branch register as per Articles 20 and 34;  
(e) Providing a detailed list of data to be transmitted for information exchange between registers, as referenced in Articles 20, 34, and 130;  
(f) Defining the structure of the standard message format for information exchange between registers, the platform, and the portal;  
(g) Specifying the data set necessary for the platform’s functions, including methods for data storage, use, and protection;  
(h) Defining the structure and use of a unique identifier for communication between registers;  
(i) Specifying the technical methods for the operation of the interconnection system regarding information distribution and exchange, including IT services ensuring message delivery in the appropriate language version;  
(j) Establishing harmonised criteria for the search service provided by the portal;  
(k) Defining payment modalities, considering available options such as online payment;  
(l) Detailing explanatory labels listing particulars and document types referred to in Article 14;  
(m) Setting technical conditions for the availability of services provided by the interconnection system;  
(n) Defining procedures and technical requirements for connecting optional access points to the platform.

All these implementing acts shall be adopted following the examination procedure specified in Article 164(2), ensuring proper oversight and coordination in their development and application.

Article 30 outlines the specific documents and particulars that must be compulsorily disclosed under the provisions of Article 29. The mandatory disclosure includes the following key elements:

(a) The address of the branch;  
(b) The activities carried out by the branch;  
(c) The register in which the company file (as referred to in Article 16) is kept, along with the registration number in that register;  
(d) The name and legal form of the company, as well as the name of the branch if it differs from the company’s name;  
(e) Details regarding the appointment and termination of office, as well as particulars of persons authorized to represent the company in dealings with third parties and in legal proceedings. This includes:  
 — persons acting as company organs constituted pursuant to law or as members of such organs, in line with the disclosure requirements of Article 14(d);  
 — permanent representatives of the company for branch activities, including the extent of their powers;  
(f) Information concerning:  
 — the winding-up of the company, appointment of liquidators, their particulars and powers, and the termination of liquidation, as disclosed by the company under Article 14(h), (j), and (k);  
 — insolvency proceedings, arrangements, compositions, or any analogous proceedings involving the company;  
(g) The accounting documents as required by Article 31;  
(h) The closure of the branch.

Furthermore, the Member State where the branch is established may require additional disclosures under Article 29, such as:  

(a) The signatures of the persons mentioned in points (e) and (f) above;  
(b) The instruments of constitution and the memorandum and articles of association, if contained in separate instruments, including any amendments, in accordance with Article 14(a), (b), and (c);  
(c) An attestation from the register referred to in point (c) confirming the existence of the company;  
(d) An indication of securities on the company’s property located in that Member State, provided such disclosure pertains to the validity of those securities.

This framework ensures transparency regarding the branch’s identity, governance, financial status, and legal standing, facilitating trust and legal certainty in cross-border business operations.
